2. Per-Character Touch Control Settings

2.1 New optional setting

In:

Home → Settings → Controls

Add an option named:

Separate controls for each character

This should be a clearly understandable On/Off setting, styled consistently with ALVA’s existing settings.

Default: Off.

When Off:

* The current behavior remains unchanged.
* All characters use the same shared touch-control customization.
* Joystick and Classic layouts remain independently customizable.
* Any previous shared settings continue working exactly as before.

When On:

* Players can configure each playable character’s touch buttons independently.
* Each character can have different button positions, sizes, and transparency.
* Each character maintains independent configurations for Joystick and Classic.
* Changing one character’s layout must never overwrite another character’s layout.
* Disabling the option must not delete saved character-specific configurations.
* Re-enabling it restores those configurations.

This feature concerns touch-control customization. The existing global Joystick/Classic selection should remain unchanged unless required by the established control system.

2.2 Character selection in the Controls menu

When separate character controls are enabled, show a character selector.

Use the actual playable roster from js/data/characters.js, rather than hardcoding character IDs.

The initial implementation must work for:

* Character 0001
* Character 0002

It must also support future playable characters automatically.

Display each character’s existing display name, with an obvious selected state.

The selector should work with mouse, touch, keyboard, and gamepad navigation.

Selecting a character here only chooses whose controls are being customized. It must not modify the player’s currently selected fighter for Quick Battle.

2.3 Character selection in the layout editor

When the player opens Customize touch controls, the editor should clearly show which profile is currently being edited.

For shared settings, show a label such as:

Shared controls — Joystick

For character-specific settings, show something like:

[Character Name] — Joystick

Provide a way to change the character directly inside the editor when per-character customization is enabled.

Switching characters should immediately load the appropriate saved layout, sizes, and transparency values.

Keep the existing neutral editor icons and stable action codenames. Character-specific gameplay artwork must continue to be handled by the existing touch-control rendering system.

All controls, including the five numbered attacks, should remain available for layout customization, even if a particular fighter doesn’t currently have an ability assigned to one of them.

2.4 Settings storage and inheritance

Extend the existing versioned settings object without destroying previously saved data.

Use an architecture conceptually equivalent to:

{
  version: 4,
  language: "en",
  mobileControls: "joystick",
  touchLayouts: {
    joystick: {},
    classic: {}
  },
  separateCharacterControls: false,
  characterTouchLayouts: {
    "0001": {
      joystick: {},
      classic: {}
    },
    "0002": {
      joystick: {},
      classic: {}
    }
  },
  combatAssist: true
}

This is an illustrative schema, not a requirement to store empty objects for every character.

Implement a clean distinction between:

* A shared layout.
* A character-specific override.
* A character with no override yet.
* An explicitly reset character-specific layout.

A character without its own customization should initially inherit the corresponding shared layout.

When the player first customizes that character, create an independent profile based on the currently effective layout.

From that point onward, its settings should be independent.

Provide an option to restore a character’s shared layout inheritance without deleting the shared layout itself.

Preserve the editor’s existing Reset to Defaults action, but make its scope clear so players understand whether they are resetting a shared layout or one character’s layout.

Do not reset every character when only one character is being edited.

2.5 Apply the correct controls during gameplay

Update the touch-control initialization in:

js/screens/battle-screen.js

and:

js/screens/practice-screen.js

The game must resolve the correct layout using:

* The current Mobile Controls scheme.
* Whether per-character customization is enabled.
* The currently controlled player character.
* That character’s saved profile, if available.
* The shared fallback configuration when applicable.

Important: Only the human player’s fighter determines the player’s control profile.

The CPU fighter must never influence touch-control placement or transparency.

Watch Mode must remain unaffected, with no human touch controls.

2.6 Practice Ground character switching

Practice Ground allows changing fighters during an active session.

Currently, PracticeGroundScreen.changeFighter() updates the fighter and the touch-button artwork.

Extend this so changing fighters also updates the effective control profile immediately.

Example:

Character 0001 has large attack buttons on the right and a smaller joystick.

Character 0002 has smaller attack buttons with different positioning and transparency.

When switching from 0001 to 0002 in Practice Ground, the correct controls must appear automatically, without restarting Practice Ground.

Switching back must restore 0001’s original configuration.

Avoid stuck inputs, stale pointer ownership, or leftover visual styles when applying another fighter’s layout.

