4. Multi-Select Buttons for Batch Editing

The current touch editor only supports a single selected control.

Extend it to support selecting multiple buttons simultaneously.

The primary goal is to resize several buttons or change their opacity together.

4.1 Multi-selection interaction

Add a clearly visible Multi-select mode in the editor toolbar.

Normal mode:

* Tap or click a control to select it.
* Drag a control to move it.
* Existing single-control editing remains intact.

Multi-select mode:

* Tap or click a button to add it to the selection.
* Tap or click it again to remove it.
* Keep previously selected buttons selected.
* Show a visible outline around each selected control.
* Indicate how many controls are selected.
* Provide a Clear Selection action.

Support keyboard and gamepad confirmation for adding and removing controls from the selection.

On desktop, Shift+click may also be supported as a convenient additive-selection shortcut.

Do not make ordinary touch dragging ambiguous. While multiple controls are selected, disable position dragging until the user returns to single-control editing, or use another clear interaction that preserves the existing drag behavior.

4.2 Multi-button resizing

The existing Size slider and Smaller/Larger controls must work on the entire selection.

Example:

The player selects Attack 1, Attack 2, Attack 3, and Jump.

They set Size to 140%.

All four controls become 140% of their normal size immediately.

For the slider, use an absolute target size applied consistently to every selected control.

For Smaller/Larger buttons, adjust each selected control by the existing size step, clamping it to the allowed range.

Keep the existing 70%–180% size limits.

If selected buttons have different sizes, display a clear Mixed state in the toolbar until the player chooses a common absolute value.

Do not unexpectedly change their positions, except for the minimum correction necessary to keep enlarged controls within the safe control area.

4.3 Multi-button transparency

The same selection must work with the new Opacity slider.

Example:

The player selects:

* Attack 1
* Attack 2
* Shield
* Jump

They set Opacity to 50%.

All four selected controls become 50% opaque.

Every unselected button remains unchanged.

If the selected buttons have different opacity values, indicate Mixed until the player applies a common opacity.

4.4 Saving batch operations

Batch changes must save into the currently active profile:

* Shared Joystick.
* Shared Classic.
* Character-specific Joystick.
* Character-specific Classic.

Apply and commit a batch change as one logical update rather than repeatedly overwriting storage for each button.

Switching the character or scheme must update the preview and selection state cleanly.

Never apply a selected button ID to an incompatible scheme.

4.5 Editor toolbar improvements

Extend the current compact editor toolbar to include:

* Active layout/character information.
* Multi-select toggle.
* Selected control name or selected count.
* Size adjustment.
* Opacity adjustment.
* Clear Selection.
* Reset to Defaults.
* Done.

Keep the interface compact and consistent with the existing glass-panel appearance.

On small landscape screens, allow sensible wrapping or compact grouping without covering the control layout excessively.

Retain working keyboard/gamepad navigation and focus restoration.

Ensure the editor still supports single-button dragging and keyboard/gamepad nudging.

